    "agent_messages": [
      "I added `POST /purchases/bulk-reject`. I only had to change `internal/candidate/approval.go`. `go build`, `go vet` and `go test` pass, but there are no test files and no database credentials, so nothing has actually been run against Postgres.\n\nWhat changed:\n- There's a new `Reject` transition: operation `bulk-reject.v4`, `submitted` → `rejected`, and the response field is `rejectedIds`.\n- A shared `handler(executor, transition)` now wraps `executor.Run` for both routes. It turns `action.Rejection` into `protocol.Rejection` at this boundary.\n- Both paths are registered in one `protocol.Routes`. `bulk-approve` now goes through the same wrapper with `action.Approve`, so its behaviour is the same as before.\n\nAlmost all of the required behaviour comes from code I didn't change. I read it to check what it does:\n- `protocol.HTTP` handles identity, the approver check, the body size limit, body parsing and `Idempotency-Key` validation. It sets `Idempotency-Replayed`, returns 409 for unique-key conflicts and 503 for other database errors.\n- `action.Executor.Run` does the rest inside one RLS-guarded transaction:\n  - It takes a lock per operation, tenant, actor and key, so different actors or tenants using the same key don't interfere.\n  - A replay of the same target set returns the stored IDs with no new writes; the same key with a different set returns 409.\n  - It locks the targets with `FOR UPDATE` and returns 409 unless every target is eligible. This is what makes overlapping batches, and an approve racing a reject on the same purchase, end with only one success.\n  - The status update, the `ApprovalAudit` rows and the one `ActionRequest` row are written together, so if any write fails the whole transaction rolls back.\n- In `SCHEMA.sql`, the v4 `purchase_decide` policy only allows updates from `submitted` to `approved` or `rejected`, so rejected purchases can't be approved or rejected again, and approved ones can't be rejected.\n\nThe kernel also writes `ApprovalAudit` rows for rejections, with the rejecting user stored in the column named `approvedBy`. The v4 schema allows this."
    ],
